  "name": "Folclore salteño",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Regional folklore of Salta province in Argentina's northwest, professionalised from the 1940s-50s by Los Chalchaleros, Eduardo Falú and the poet Manuel J. Castilla, whose repertoire centres on the slow 6/8 zamba with its rubato pañuelo phrasing, the fast chacarera and the bagualas of the valleys sung with caja over a major-triad three-note scale, scored for close male four-part vocal harmony with two or three guitars, bombo legüero and occasional erke or quena colour.",
  "recipe": "classic South American mid-1940s folclore salteño, ballad Poetry, Latin am troubadour branch, Latin am groove percussion, andean carnival processional huayno peruano bolivian diablada de oruro. vocal fry, breath veiled tone, fast rate, interlocked note trading. cedar pre 1940 period gut fingerpicked classical nylon-string guitar, swaying bombo leguero, pre hispanic bone quena, naranjillo andean premium wood cedar andean andean classical charango. adobe room. RCA 44-BX, German V72 tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
